# a scheduled module as one relocatable wasm object, stated through masc's wasm module maker as mink's neutral object
#
# the object is a valid module binary with a "linking" section, as tool-conventions' Linking.md states, which mink
# writes from the neutral object and links into a final module. its functions another module defines are imported from
# the module the environment names for its imports, and what the link defines by the name mink's link gives it
# (mink.link.wasm.made): its linear memory as env.__linear_memory, its stack pointer as env.__stack_pointer, and
# where a body calls through the table, the table as env.__indirect_function_table. without Linking.md's
# call-indirect-overlong feature a call_indirect's table is a single byte no relocation reaches, so the table is named
# by its import alone. every global the module defines is a data segment of its own, named by its section or by the
# kind of section its placement takes, then a dot and its name, laid out from 0 so each has an address in the object's
# own layout, and a global another module defines is an undefined data symbol. the object's symbols are every function,
# every global and the stack pointer in the order mirl.target.wasm.relocation gives, each bound and shown by its
# linkage, a function another module defines and the stack pointer naming their imports. every symbolic operand of a
# body and every address in an initial value is a relocation of its body's or segment's section at its offset there,
# of the neutral kind of its fixup's site. a symbol the module marks exported has an export directive, and the object
# has no export, table or element segment of its own, as the link lays them out. the target features the code uses are
# the object's wasm-features trait, and where the module states source files the object states the line records of
# its bodies (mirl.target.wasm.line), which mink lowers to a DWARF line table.

